@title Search-as-you-type: implementation plan
@subtitle Debounced input with request cancellation for the product search box in the React app
@author Claude, Frontend platform
@date 2026-10-19
@width wide

## TL;DR

Today the search box fires one request per keystroke and shows whichever response lands last. This plan adds a 250 ms debounce and cancels the superseded request with `AbortController`, so results always match what is in the box.

kpi Requests per search | 3 | -73% vs. 11 today | good
kpi Debounce | 250 ms | trial range 150 to 400 ms | neutral
kpi Estimate | 8 days | 2 engineers | neutral
kpi Files touched | 7 | 3 new | neutral

- **In scope:** the header search box, the `useSearch` hook, the fetch client, tests, a feature flag.
- **Out of scope:** server-side ranking, search history, keyboard navigation of results.
- **Done means:** typing "react hooks" never shows results for "react ho", and the Network tab shows aborted requests rather than parallel ones.

## What the user sees

Four states of the search box. The key rule: old results stay on screen while a new request is loading, so the list never flashes empty.

grid 2 count=4
```
IDLE (fewer than 2 characters)
+-----------------------------+
| (search) Search products... |
+-----------------------------+
 Type 2 or more characters
```
```
LOADING (old results stay visible)
+-----------------------------+
| (search) react ho      (...)|
+-----------------------------+
 React Hooks Handbook   (dim)
 React Hooks in Depth   (dim)
```
```
RESULTS
+-----------------------------+
| (search) react hooks     (x)|
+-----------------------------+
 React Hooks Handbook
 React Hooks in Depth
 Hooks Cheat Sheet        3 hits
```
```
EMPTY and ERROR
+-----------------------------+
| (search) zzzq            (x)|
+-----------------------------+
 No results for "zzzq"

 Error: Search failed (503)
 [ Retry ]
```

## How the data flows

Everything between the keystroke and the screen runs in the browser except one request. The hook `useSearch` is the only place that owns an `AbortController`.

diagram flow dir=LR title="Keystroke to results"
flow: user "User" user
flow: box "SearchBox" fn "controlled input"
flow: deb "useDebouncedValue" fn "250 ms idle"
flow: hook "useSearch" fn "owns AbortController" focus
flow: cache "Query cache" cache "Map, 60 s TTL"
flow: list "ResultsList" fn
flow: api "GET /api/search" svc "existing endpoint"
flow: group app "React app (browser)" box, deb, hook, cache, list
flow: user -> box : keystrokes
flow: box -> deb : raw text
flow: deb -> hook : settled query
flow: hook -> cache : lookup
flow: cache --> list : hit
flow: hook -> api : fetch + signal
flow: api --> hook : JSON
flow: hook -> list : items, status

### What happens when two queries overlap

The case this feature exists for: the user keeps typing while request A is still in flight.

diagram race sequence title="Typing past an in-flight request"
race: user "User" user
race: box "SearchBox" fn
race: hook "useSearch" fn
race: api "Search API" svc
race: user -> box : types re
race: box -> hook : query re after 250 ms idle
race: hook -> api : GET q=re (request A)
race: user -> box : types a
race: box -> hook : query rea after 250 ms idle
race: note hook : effect cleanup calls A.abort()
race: hook --> api : abort A
race: hook -> api : GET q=rea (request B)
race: opt "A was already in flight"
race: api --> hook : A rejects with AbortError
race: note hook : ignored, state untouched
race: end
race: api --> hook : B returns 200 with items
race: hook --> box : status ok, items from B

## Decisions worth a look

### Debounce delay

data deb = delay,requests,added wait ms; "0 ms",11,0; "150 ms",6,150; "250 ms",3,250; "400 ms",2,400

Requests fired while typing "react hooks" in a recorded burst-typing session.

chart bar deb x=delay y=requests title="Requests per search by debounce delay" focus="250 ms"

!! The delay is the latency floor. 250 ms is the usual point where requests drop sharply without the box feeling laggy. Make it a constant and revisit after a week of real traffic.

### Other choices

table = decision,chosen,rejected,why; Cancellation,`AbortController` per effect,"ignore-stale flag only","Cancels the network request as well as the update"; Debounce,"hook on the value","debounced callback","Works with React 18 Strict Mode double effects"; Cache,"in-memory `Map`, 60 s","SWR or React Query","No new dependency for one box"; Min length,2 characters,1 character,"One letter matches most of the catalog"

## Code to review

### 1. The debounce hook

Debounce the value, not the handler. The cleanup clears the timer on every change, which is the whole trick.

```ts
// src/hooks/useDebouncedValue.ts
import { useEffect, useState } from 'react';

export function useDebouncedValue<T>(value: T, delayMs = 250): T {
  const [debounced, setDebounced] = useState(value);

  useEffect(() => {
    const id = setTimeout(() => setDebounced(value), delayMs);
    return () => clearTimeout(id);
  }, [value, delayMs]);

  return debounced;
}
```

### 2. The search hook with cancellation

The cleanup function aborts the previous request. The `AbortError` guard stops a cancelled request from being reported as a failure.

```ts
// src/hooks/useSearch.ts
const MIN_CHARS = 2;

export function useSearch(query: string): SearchState {
  const [state, setState] = useState<SearchState>({ status: 'idle', items: [] });

  useEffect(() => {
    const q = query.trim();
    if (q.length < MIN_CHARS) {
      setState({ status: 'idle', items: [] });
      return undefined;
    }

    const controller = new AbortController();
    setState((s) => ({ ...s, status: 'loading' })); // keep old items on screen

    searchApi(q, controller.signal)
      .then((items) =>
        setState({ status: items.length ? 'ok' : 'empty', items }),
      )
      .catch((err) => {
        if (err.name === 'AbortError') return; // superseded, not a failure
        setState({ status: 'error', items: [], error: err.message });
      });

    return () => controller.abort();
  }, [query]);

  return state;
}
```

### 3. The fetch client

```ts
// src/api/search.ts
export async function searchApi(q: string, signal: AbortSignal): Promise<Item[]> {
  const res = await fetch(`/api/search?q=${encodeURIComponent(q)}`, { signal });
  if (!res.ok) throw new Error(`Search failed (${res.status})`);
  return (await res.json()).items;
}
```

### 4. The component

```tsx
// src/components/SearchBox.tsx
export function SearchBox() {
  const [text, setText] = useState('');
  const debounced = useDebouncedValue(text, 250);
  const { status, items, error } = useSearch(debounced);

  return (
    <div role="search">
      <input
        value={text}
        onChange={(e) => setText(e.target.value)}
        aria-busy={status === 'loading'}
        aria-controls="search-results"
      />
      <ResultsList id="search-results" status={status} items={items} error={error} />
    </div>
  );
}
```

### 5. The test that proves cancellation

```ts
// src/hooks/useSearch.test.ts
it('aborts the previous request when the query changes', async () => {
  const signals: AbortSignal[] = [];
  vi.spyOn(api, 'searchApi').mockImplementation((_q, signal) => {
    signals.push(signal);
    return new Promise(() => {}); // never resolves
  });

  const { rerender } = renderHook(({ q }) => useSearch(q), {
    initialProps: { q: 're' },
  });
  rerender({ q: 'rea' });

  expect(signals).toHaveLength(2);
  expect(signals[0].aborted).toBe(true);
  expect(signals[1].aborted).toBe(false);
});
```

